==English==

===Noun===
{{en-noun|-|head=[[human]] [[resource]]s}}
The role of HR is often misunderstood. Common misconceptions include that:

1.	 HR is a management decision maker that decides who gets hired and fired. This is untrue. HR may provide advice to management in relation to these processes but is not the decision maker. 

2.	Another common misconception is that HRs role is that of employee bargaining representative when in fact, the role of HR is primarily to provide the employer (the company) with advice, guidance and programs that relate to employment of it’s workforce. 

The role of a modern HR function is to support and advise company management in relation to workforce matters. This is a delicate balance in that HR must also maintain some impartiality especially in dealing with any serious employee complaints. Some of the activities HR may be involved in within an organisation include but are not limited to:
-	Supporting hiring managers with the recruitment process
-	Facilitating induction and on-boarding processes
-	Developing HR policies
-	Gathering data from exit interviews
-	Managing HR information systems
-	Monitoring HR analytics and reporting to management regarding turnover, absenteeism, leave liability, headcount and recruitment metrics
-	Maintaining job descriptions
-	Supporting health and wellbeing initiatives
-	Assisting management with the development of diversity and inclusion plans
-	Assisting with implementing leadership development programs
-	Supporting benchmarking processes and remuneration review processes
-	Facilitating employee engagement surveys and reporting results to management
-	Assisting management with the handling of grievances and complaints
-	Interpreting industrial instruments 
-	Payroll related activities
-	Providing management with advice related to employment legislation
-	Preparing contracts of employment
-	Maintaining personnel files
-	Facilitating training and development initiatives



